Return ONE JSON object (no markdown, no code fences).

You are given a CASE DIGEST describing an SMT-vs-LLM eligibility disagreement case.
Goal: produce a compact, evidence-grounded “issue card” that is comparable across many cases.
Do NOT invent facts.

IMPORTANT: In addition to "likely_fault", you must classify the PRIMARY FIX DOMAIN:
- value_miner: errors in extracting/mapping patient values to SMT variables (LLM miner / value miner / var binding)
- solver_logic: errors in evaluation/aggregation logic (polarity, unknown handling, OR-over-subcohorts, parent_or, etc.)
- smt_translation: SMT encoding/translation mistakes (criteria → SMT constraints incorrect/incomplete)
- llm_judge: judge prompt/model reasoning mistakes (misreads criteria, ignores evidence, wrong polarity, etc.)
- mixed: truly multiple domains
- unclear: insufficient evidence

Keep outputs short and evidence-grounded.

Schema (exact keys; do not add/remove keys):
{
  "issue_statement": "1-2 sentences describing what went wrong (plain English)",
  "what_llm_vs_smt_disagreed_on": "short; e.g., LLM=eligible vs SMT(incl/excl)=... or parent_or=...",
  "primary_fix_domain": "value_miner" | "solver_logic" | "smt_translation" | "llm_judge" | "mixed" | "unclear",
  "secondary_fix_domains": ["value_miner" | "solver_logic" | "smt_translation" | "llm_judge"],
  "likely_fault": "llm_judge" | "smt_pipeline" | "both" | "unclear",
  "mechanism_hypotheses": ["...", "..."],
  "evidence_snippets": [{"source":"...", "snippet":"..."}],
  "fix_ideas": [{"where":"...", "action":"..."}],
  "keywords": ["...", "...", "..."]
}

Rules:
- primary_fix_domain should be your best single call; use mixed if truly multi-causal.
- secondary_fix_domains can be empty.
- evidence_snippets: 1–5 items, each snippet should be SHORT.
- fix_ideas: 2–8 items is OK; each should point to a concrete location (prompt vs mining vs evaluator vs encoding).
- If key info is missing, say so explicitly (don’t guess).
